1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,184
Take twelve parts off one machine. Same tool,

2
00:00:04,284 --> 00:00:08,468
same operator, same gauge, one after another.

3
00:00:08,566 --> 00:00:12,382
Every reading is different, and nothing is broken.

4
00:00:12,482 --> 00:00:15,906
The twelve of them cover forty seven microns.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,166
Two of them differ by a single micron, which is exactly

6
00:00:20,266 --> 00:00:24,588
where this gauge stops. Below that it has nothing to say.

7
00:00:24,683 --> 00:00:28,223
Spread is not a defect. It is what every real

8
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:31,783
process does, and the job is to describe it.

9
00:00:31,883 --> 00:00:35,874
Twelve numbers are not an answer. You need one number

10
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:39,271
for the centre — so guess one, and check it.

11
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:43,594
Here is a guess, with every part's distance to it drawn

12
00:00:43,694 --> 00:00:47,607
in. Add the distances on each side and the guess is

13
00:00:47,707 --> 00:00:51,698
out: the two sides do not cancel, and the beam tips.

14
00:00:51,783 --> 00:00:56,070
So walk the guess along until they do. One position makes

15
00:00:56,170 --> 00:01:00,611
the two sides equal and opposite, and the beam comes level.

16
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,527
That position is the mean. Not a formula you were

17
00:01:04,627 --> 00:01:08,616
handed — the one place where the deviations cancel.

18
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:13,397
Now the spread. Averaging those deviations is useless:

19
00:01:13,497 --> 00:01:16,900
we just proved they cancel, by construction.

20
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,658
Square each deviation instead. Negatives turn positive, the cancelling stops, and

21
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:27,580
every square is an area you can put on a shelf.

22
00:01:27,666 --> 00:01:34,004
Twelve squares averaged, then rooted, which puts the answer back in millimetres.

23
00:01:34,104 --> 00:01:38,510
That is sigma, and it is the side of the average square.

24
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:42,628
For these twelve parts, 12.7 microns.

25
00:01:43,216 --> 00:01:46,472
Twelve parts say nothing about shape. Keep

26
00:01:46,572 --> 00:01:49,188
measuring, and keep the same bins.

27
00:01:49,283 --> 00:01:53,005
A hundred parts. A thousand. Twenty thousand, off

28
00:01:53,105 --> 00:01:56,671
the same machine, measured with the same gauge.

29
00:01:56,766 --> 00:02:00,506
And a shape appears that nobody chose or asked for.

30
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:06,816
Many small independent effects, added together, land on this curve. The bell

31
00:02:06,916 --> 00:02:11,804
is a consequence of the process, not an assumption about it.

32
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:17,407
One more thing, and then Level 1. You never measure everything.

33
00:02:17,507 --> 00:02:21,948
The true centre and spread belong to the process itself.

34
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:24,746
Your twelve parts only estimate them, and

35
00:02:24,846 --> 00:02:26,805
the estimate is not the thing.

36
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:29,955
Four more handfuls of twelve, off the same

37
00:02:30,055 --> 00:02:33,712
untouched machine, land somewhere else every time.

38
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,767
It is also why a sample's spread divides by n minus one.

39
00:02:37,867 --> 00:02:42,634
Twelve parts spread around their own mean sit a little tighter than

40
00:02:42,734 --> 00:02:47,356
they do around the truth, so the smaller divisor corrects for it.

41
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:51,509
Every estimate carries uncertainty. Level 1 puts

42
00:02:51,609 --> 00:02:54,022
a number on exactly how much.

